      <div class="prose prose-lg prose-blue max-w-none text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
        <div id="how-we-chose" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">3. How We Chose These Treadmills</h2>
<p>
            We have not tested these treadmills, and nothing on this page should be read as if we had.
            The picks were made from owner's manuals, warranty terms, specification sheets, product
            pages and retailer listings, read on 15 October 2026.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>The deciding rule was the paperwork.</strong> A garage is a different environment
            from a living room, and the most expensive surprise for a garage owner is a warranty claim
            refused on location grounds. So we read the environment and warranty sections of current
            manuals and only recommended a machine where the manual we read did not exclude garage use,
            or — in one labelled case — where the build is strong enough to consider and we could not
            find the maker's terms. That rule removed some excellent treadmills:
          </p>
          <ul>
            <li><strong>Sole.</strong> The 2026 owner's manual for the Sole F80 lists among its warranty
            exclusions: "Warranty will be void if the unit is not in a climate-controlled, insulated
            environment. (Examples: Garage, outside, carport, etc)". We read only the F80's manual, but
            we would not assume other Sole models differ without seeing theirs.</li>
            <li><strong>Bowflex.</strong> The T6 and T9 owner's manuals say the equipment "must be used in
            an environment that is both temperature and humidity controlled" and name garages, car
            ports and porches among the places not to use it, adding: "Failure to comply will void the
            warranty."</li>
            <li><strong>TRUE.</strong> The Performance series manual we read does not name garages, but
            it says not to use the treadmill "in areas of high humidity including extreme temperature
            changes" — a fair description of many garages — and the retailer we checked sold these
            models as a base only, with the console separate, so we could not give a complete
            price.</li>
            <li><strong>AssaultRunner.</strong> Assault's own page for the motorless AssaultRunner Pro
            talks about the garage and the driveway, and the Elite lists a rust-resistant zinc-rich
            primer — a genuine garage design. But all three AssaultRunner models were shown as sold
            out on Assault's site on 15 October, so they are not here.</li>
            <li><strong>Matrix.</strong> In January 2026 the CPSC announced an expanded fire-hazard recall
            covering Matrix retail treadmills including the T50, for certain serial numbers made through
            January 2024. Current units may well be unaffected, but we could not confirm which serials
            are on sale, so we left Matrix off.</li>
            <li><strong>Woodway Curve LTG.</strong> A motorless curved trainer that needs no outlet, but
            Woodway lists it as price on request, and we do not list a machine without a price we have
            seen.</li>
          </ul>
          <p>
            We could not open current manuals for several other major brands on the day, including
            Horizon and NordicTrack, so their absence is not a judgement on their garage terms — it means
            we could not check them. We also searched the CPSC recall database for every brand on this
            page and found no recall covering these models. Finally, the four Spirit machines deserve an
            explanation: it is not a preference for the brand, it is that Spirit was the only maker
            whose current manuals we could read that state an environmental specification and leave
            garages out of the warranty exclusions.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="what-manuals-say" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">4. What the Manuals Actually Say About Garages</h2>
<p>
            Here is what we read, quoted or closely paraphrased, for the specific documents named. Terms
            change between manual revisions, so treat this as a guide to what to look for in the manual
            for your unit, not as a substitute for it.
          </p>
          <div class="not-prose overflow-x-auto my-8"><table class="min-w-[480px] w-full text-sm border-collapse">
            <thead>
              <tr>
                <th class="text-left font-black text-gray-900 border-b border-gray-200 py-2 pr-4">Document</th>
                <th class="text-left font-black text-gray-900 border-b border-gray-200 py-2 pr-4">Garage and environment</th>
                <th class="text-left font-black text-gray-900 border-b border-gray-200 py-2 pr-4">Power</th>
              </tr>
            </thead>
            <tbody>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Spirit XT185, XT285, XT385, XT685 manuals (2025–2026 revisions)</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Not for outdoors, near a pool or spa, or "any other high humidity environment"; temperature specification 40 degrees C; humidity 95% non-condensing. Garages not named in the warranty exclusions; corrosion is excluded.</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Avoid AFCI/GFCI breakers "if possible"; ideally the only device on the circuit</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Sole F80 manual (2026)</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Maximum operating temperature 40 degrees C, humidity 95% non-condensing; warranty void outside a climate-controlled, insulated environment, garage given as an example; rust excluded</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">15-amp grounded outlet, treadmill alone on the circuit; avoid AFCI/GFCI if possible</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Bowflex T6 and T9 manuals</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Must be used where temperature and humidity are controlled; not in garages, car ports or porches; non-compliance voids the warranty</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Dedicated circuit, minimum 15 amps; "not compatible with GFCI-equipped outlets"</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">TRUE Performance series manual</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Not outdoors, near water, while wet, or "in areas of high humidity including extreme temperature changes"</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Dedicated line without GFCI or AFCI; some models need 20 amps</td></tr>
            </tbody>
          </table></div>
          <p>
            Three things stand out. First, <strong>no manual we read gives a minimum temperature.</strong>
            The 40 degrees C figure in the Spirit and Sole manuals is an upper limit, roughly 104
            degrees F, which a closed garage in a hot summer can approach. Silence about the bottom of
            the range is not permission to run the machine below freezing.
          </p>
          <p>
            Second, <strong>"non-condensing" is the whole garage problem.</strong> Condensation forms
            when warm, humid air meets a cold machine — opening the garage door on a mild morning after a
            cold night is the classic case. A treadmill whose manual allows 95% humidity
            non-condensing is still not designed to have water forming on its electronics.
          </p>
          <p>
            Third, <strong>rust is your problem everywhere.</strong> Spirit, Sole and Bowflex all exclude
            corrosion or rust from their warranties. A garage-friendly warranty protects you against
            the motor or board failing; it does not protect the frame from a damp winter. Our
            <a href="/treadmill-warranty-guide/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">treadmill warranty guide</a>
            explains how to read the rest of the exclusions list.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="cold-and-condensation" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">5. Cold, Heat and Condensation: Keeping the Machine in Range</h2>
<p>
            Even with a warranty that tolerates a garage, the machine itself has limits. The practical
            goal is to keep the space dry, above freezing and below the upper temperature figure in the
            manual, and to avoid rapid swings.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>In winter,</strong> cold stiffens the belt and the deck lubricant, so the motor works
            harder in the first minutes, and plastic parts become more brittle. Warm the space before
            you train rather than starting the treadmill cold, then run the belt empty at a slow speed for
            a few minutes before you step on. If the machine has been colder than the room, give it time
            to come up to temperature and check it is dry before switching on. Spirit's troubleshooting
            table also links a console that shuts off when touched "on a cold day" to static and
            grounding; our guide to
            <a href="/treadmill-static-shock/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">treadmill static shock</a>
            covers why dry winter air makes it worse.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>In summer,</strong> a closed garage can get very hot. The motor and controller shed
            heat into the air around them, and they cool less effectively in hot air. Ventilate, train
            at the cooler end of the day, and keep the motor hood clear. Our
            <a href="/treadmill-overheating/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">treadmill overheating guide</a>
            covers the warning signs.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Simple consoles versus big screens</h3>
          <p>
            Every pick on this page has a simple LCD console rather than a large built-in touchscreen,
            and that is deliberate. None of the manuals we read gives the console its own environmental
            rating, so we cannot tell you a touchscreen fails sooner in the cold. But the reasoning is
            straightforward: a garage is harder on electronics than a living room, and a machine with
            less of them — and whose console is cheap to replace — has less to lose. A tablet on a holder
            can live indoors between sessions; a 22-inch screen bolted to the treadmill cannot.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="rust-and-humidity" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">6. Rust, Humidity and Covers</h2>
<p>
            Since the Spirit, Sole and Bowflex warranties we read all exclude corrosion, keeping the frame dry is on
            you. The good news is that it is mostly habit.
          </p>
          <ul>
            <li><strong>Measure the humidity.</strong> A cheap hygrometer tells you whether your garage
            is damp or merely feels it. If it stays high, a dehumidifier on a suitable circuit is the
            single most useful garage-gym purchase.</li>
            <li><strong>Wipe sweat off after every session.</strong> Sweat is salty, and salt on metal in
            a damp garage is how rust starts. Pay attention to the handrails and the frame under the
            console.</li>
            <li><strong>Keep the car's wet out of it.</strong> A car brought in after rain or snow puts
            water into the air; park it away from the treadmill, or let the space dry before training.</li>
            <li><strong>Use a breathable cover,</strong> not a plastic sheet. A cover keeps dust and
            drips off; an airtight one can trap moisture against the frame. Our roundup of the
            <a href="/best-treadmill-covers/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">best treadmill covers</a>
            looks at the difference.</li>
            <li><strong>Treat surface rust early</strong> with a light abrasive and touch-up paint before
            it spreads under the coating.</li>
          </ul>
          <p>
            Frames matter too. Spirit describes powder-coated frames on its specification sheets, and
            a retailer lists the XT685's as commercial-grade structural steel with powder-coat paint.
            A good coating slows rust; it does not stop it where the coating is chipped, so check the
            frame when it arrives and touch up any transit damage.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="dust-and-floor" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">7. Dust, the Floor and Maintenance</h2>
<p>
            Garages are dusty, and dust is a treadmill's slow enemy. It gets drawn into the motor
            compartment by the cooling fan, it settles on the belt and deck, and it turns lubricant into
            something closer to paste.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Put a mat under it.</strong> On bare concrete a mat reduces the dust drawn up from
            the floor, protects the floor from the feet, deadens noise and gives a little thermal break
            between a cold slab and the frame. Our guide to
            <a href="/do-you-need-a-treadmill-mat/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">whether you need a treadmill mat</a>
            covers sizes and thickness.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Vacuum the motor compartment more often than the manual's indoor schedule
            suggests.</strong> Unplug the machine first, remove the hood as the manual describes, and
            clear dust from the motor, controller and fan. Our step-by-step guide to
            <a href="/how-to-vacuum-treadmill-motor-compartment/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">vacuuming the motor compartment</a>
            shows how. Spirit's manuals name dirt, dust and hair blocking air inlets among the things to
            watch.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Check the belt and deck regularly.</strong> Dust and cold both increase friction
            between belt and deck, and higher friction means higher current draw — which in a garage
            often shows up as a tripping breaker. Several of the XT685's troubleshooting entries point
            to belt and deck friction and cleaning before anything else. Our
            <a href="/treadmill-belt-lubrication/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">belt lubrication guide</a>
            covers the deck side of that, and the manual is the authority on what each model needs —
            some Spirit decks are waxed and maintenance-free rather than lubricated by hand.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Mind the pests.</strong> Garages bring mice, and mice like the warm, sheltered space
            under a motor hood. A cover, a clean floor and an occasional look under the hood are the
            defence.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="garage-power" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">8. Garage Power: Dedicated Circuits, GFCI and Extension Cords</h2>
<p>
            Power is where garages differ most from the rest of the house, and where manuals disagree
            most with garage wiring. Unplug the treadmill before any inspection, and get a licensed
            electrician for anything involving the circuit itself.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Give the treadmill its own circuit.</strong> Every manual we read wants the
            treadmill alone on the circuit, or as close to it as possible. Garage circuits often also
            feed the door opener, lights, a freezer, power tools or a heater, and a treadmill under load
            on top of those is a common reason for nuisance tripping. Our guide to a
            <a href="/treadmill-trips-breaker/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">treadmill that trips the breaker</a>
            explains how to tell a circuit problem from a machine problem.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>GFCI is the hard part.</strong> Garage outlets are commonly ground-fault protected,
            and treadmill motors and drives can trip those devices. The manuals handle this differently.
            Bowflex says its T6 and T9 are "not compatible with GFCI-equipped outlets". TRUE says its
            Performance treadmills need a dedicated line without GFCI or AFCI protection. Spirit and
            Sole say to avoid AFCI/GFCI breakers "if possible", note that local rules may require them,
            and name breaker ranges they say did not trip in their own testing when the treadmill was
            alone on the circuit. Whatever your manual says, <strong>never remove or bypass a GFCI to
            stop it tripping.</strong> The right fix is a properly installed dedicated circuit that meets
            your local code, which is a conversation for an electrician, not a workaround.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Avoid extension cords.</strong> Sole's F80 manual allows one only if it is 14 AWG
            or heavier with a single outlet; Spirit's XT685 troubleshooting table says not to use one
            and, if one is unavoidable, to keep it short and heavy-duty; Bowflex says not to use one or a
            power strip. Our
            <a href="/treadmill-extension-cord/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">treadmill extension cord guide</a>
            explains why, and our
            <a href="/treadmill-surge-protector/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">surge protector guide</a>
            covers the separate question of protecting the console, noting that some manuals, including
            Bowflex's, say not to plug the machine into one.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="folding-or-not" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">9. Folding or Non-Folding in a Garage</h2>
<p>
            Most garage gyms have more floor space than a spare bedroom, which tilts the choice towards a
            non-folding machine — but only if the treadmill does not have to share the space with a car.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Non-folding</strong> machines such as the XT685 and Run CX have no hinge or lift
            mechanism to stiffen or corrode, usually a heavier frame, and nothing to wrestle with in a
            cold space. They need their full footprint permanently, plus clearance: the XT685 manual
            recommends at least 6 feet behind the treadmill and 20 inches at the front and sides. Our
            <a href="/best-non-folding-treadmills/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">best non-folding treadmills</a>
            roundup covers more of them.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Folding</strong> machines such as the XT385, XT285 and XT185 earn their place where a
            car still has to fit. Spirit's folders have lift assist, and the XT385 folds to 43 inches
            long. Folded, a treadmill stands tall and narrow, so check the garage door's path and any
            storage racks, and fold it only when the belt has stopped and the deck is dry.
          </p>
          <p>
            Either way, think about how you get it there. Treadmills arrive in long, heavy boxes, and a
            garage is often the easy delivery route; our
            <a href="/treadmill-delivery-guide/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">treadmill delivery guide</a>
            covers what to ask the retailer.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="who-should-skip" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">10. Who Should Buy Something Else</h2>
<p>
            A motorised treadmill with garage-tolerant terms is a good choice for a garage you can keep
            dry and roughly within a living-space temperature range. It is the wrong choice in several
            common situations.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>If your garage regularly freezes and you will not heat it,</strong> a motorised
            treadmill is a poor fit whatever the warranty says. A motorless trainer has no motor
            controller to suffer condensation; its console usually runs on batteries. Our roundups of
            <a href="/best-curved-treadmills/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">curved treadmills</a>
            and <a href="/best-manual-treadmills/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">manual treadmills</a>
            cover the options. Frames and slats still rust, so the humidity advice still applies.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>If the plan is a porch, patio or carport,</strong> that is a different and harder
            case: every manual we read rules out outdoor use. See our guide to
            <a href="/outdoor-treadmill/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">using a treadmill outdoors</a>.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>If you have your heart set on a brand whose manual excludes garages,</strong> such
            as Bowflex or Sole, the honest options are to put it indoors, or to accept that a fault may
            not be covered. Do not rely on a salesperson's verbal reassurance; ask the manufacturer to
            confirm in writing for your model and your space.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>If you mostly walk and space is tight,</strong> a full treadmill in the garage may be
            the wrong tool. A walking pad you store indoors and use in a room you already heat avoids the
            whole problem; our <a href="/walking-pad-buying-guide/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">walking pad buying guide</a>
            is the place to start.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- FAQ -->
        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">11. Frequently Asked Questions</h2>
          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Does putting a treadmill in a garage void the warranty?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                It depends on the brand and the manual. The 2026 Sole F80 manual says the warranty is void outside a climate-controlled, insulated environment and gives a garage as an example, and the Bowflex T6 and T9 manuals say garage use voids it. Spirit's current manuals we read do not name garages. Read the environment section and the exclusions in the manual for your exact model.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can a treadmill be kept in an unheated garage?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                It can be stored there, but running a motorised treadmill in a garage that freezes is hard on it. Cold stiffens the belt and lubricant and raises the load at start-up, and warming a cold machine can cause condensation on the electronics. None of the manuals we read gives a minimum temperature. Warm the space before use, or consider a motorless trainer.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Will a treadmill trip a GFCI outlet in the garage?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                It can. Treadmill motors and drives can trip ground-fault devices, and garage outlets are commonly GFCI-protected. Bowflex says its T6 and T9 are not compatible with GFCI outlets, while Spirit and Sole say to avoid them if possible. Never bypass a GFCI. Have an electrician install a dedicated circuit that meets your local code instead.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What is the best treadmill for a garage gym?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                On the terms we read, the Spirit XT685: non-folding, rated to 425 lb, with a simple LCD console and a current manual that gives a temperature and humidity specification without excluding garages from the warranty. If a car shares the space, the folding Spirit XT385 is the alternative. Whatever you buy, keep the garage dry and above freezing.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How do I stop my garage treadmill from rusting?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Keep the air dry, ideally with a dehumidifier if a hygrometer shows high humidity. Wipe sweat off the frame and handrails after every session, park a wet car away from it, use a breathable cover rather than plastic sheeting, and treat surface rust early. The Spirit, Sole and Bowflex warranties we read all exclude corrosion, so prevention is your job.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is a folding or non-folding treadmill better for a garage?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Non-folding if the treadmill has its own space: no hinge or lift mechanism to stiffen in the cold, and usually a heavier frame. Folding if a car still has to fit; check the folded height against the garage door and racks. Either way, leave clearance behind the deck — the Spirit XT685 manual recommends at least 6 feet.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div class="mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">Common Mistakes Putting a Treadmill in a Garage</h2>
          <p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">One of these can cost you the warranty before you have used the machine, and three shorten its life once it is there.</p>
          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Not reading the warranty exclusions first</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">Some major brands void the warranty for garage use, and the clause sits in the manual rather than on the product page. Download the manual for the exact model before you buy, read the environment section and the exclusions list, and if anything is unclear, get the manufacturer’s answer in writing for your space.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Starting a cold machine at speed</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">In a cold garage the belt and lubricant are stiff and the motor works hardest in the first minutes. Warm the space, check the machine is dry, then run the belt empty at a slow speed for a few minutes before stepping on. If water has formed on it, wait until it has dried before switching on.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Plugging into a shared or GFCI garage outlet and hoping</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">A treadmill sharing a circuit with a freezer, door opener or heater is a common cause of tripping, and some manuals say their machines are not compatible with GFCI outlets. Never bypass the GFCI and do not use a light-duty extension cord. Have a dedicated circuit installed to code.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Sealing it under a plastic sheet</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">An airtight plastic cover traps moisture against the frame and electronics, which is the opposite of what a garage treadmill needs. Use a breathable cover, keep the air dry with a dehumidifier if humidity is high, and wipe sweat off after each session, because none of the Spirit, Sole or Bowflex warranties we read covers corrosion.</p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

